WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060
(audience applauding)
- My next guest grew up

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:03.610
in Ireland and fell in love

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.570
with fashion at a very young age.

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.480
But at three foot five
clothes from pages of Vogue

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.790
were not exactly meant
for someone her size.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.560
So she set out to change that.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.210
She started a blog
which led to a TED talk,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.700
which then led to rubbing elbows

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.500
with everyone from
Oprah to Kim Kardashian.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.687
Her message is simple,

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:22.971
"The world should be
designed for everyone."

00:00:22.971 --> 00:00:25.070
(audience applauding)
Activist, author, educator

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.340
and podcaster, Sinead Burke yeah.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.740
So you look cute and all those
little lips and lipstick,

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.160
I love that, all of it.
- Thank you.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.250
- So your family played a pretty big part

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.370
in your activism, Correct?

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.360
- Yeah, well firstly, this is product.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.400
(interviewer and audience laughing)

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.233
But secondly--

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:46.070
- Not pro doe' which you can get

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.680
- on another Street
- I know..

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.970
But yeah, if I'm in any way a success,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.950
it's because I'm a love child.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.580
And I grew up with this
very visceral understanding

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.840
of I could do whatever
it is that I wanted.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.000
So in my family, I'm the
oldest of five children.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.040
I have three sisters and one brother,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.430
all of whom are average height

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:04.750
like everybody else on the sofa.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.500
But my dad is a little
person, just like me.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.850
And growing up and having
somebody there who looked

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.750
so much like me really
underlying this thing that,

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.840
anything was possible,
because my dad survived

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.900
and thrived and he was just
this amazing individual

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.360
who always said that I could do

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.350
whatever and I always
wanted to be a teacher.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.840
I started out my career as
an elementary school teacher.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.130
And while I was in
university, we started a blog.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.780
And the idea of it was that as a teacher,

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.700
you would write this blog
because when your kids come home

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.067
from school, you say to them,

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:32.070
"what did you do today?"

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.100
And your child says, "nothing."

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.500
- Exactly a girl,
- But as somebody who knows--

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:37.790
- I'm like, "what is she doing all day?"

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:38.780
She never tells me.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.220
- And the lecture and
surprises that you can write

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.810
about anything you want in this blog.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:43.910
So I wrote about Cate Blanchett

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:45.960
wearing javante contort to the Oscars.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.630
because I had always this
innate interest in fashion

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.640
because as somebody who
was a disabled woman,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.700
if I sit across from you
in a product SO Dress

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:53.780
that's from a recent collection,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.460
you're like, "Oh, she's interesting."

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.020
And yet, fashion was never
considerate of people like me.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.540
As the eldest of all of my sisters,

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:02.910
I would bring them shopping,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.550
they would pick up a pair
of high heels in the store,

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:06.840
and I would try and wear them too.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.390
And they were like, "no,
this doesn't fit you."

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.650
And I remember just
thinking this was so unjust,

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.150
not only because I was more
interested in the industry,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.150
but because we legally
each have to wear clothes.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.830
And if this is something that we all have

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.433
to subscribe to,
- Not in some states.

00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:23.689
(everyone laughing)

00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:25.522
- Thankfully amolish.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.210
But why do we not all get to participate

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.330
because it's part of our
identity, it touches our skin.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.690
So this blog that evolved today
I'm getting to do a TED talk

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.490
to talk about why design should
be inclusive for everybody.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.210
That it wasn't just
something that was facetious

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.680
but it could change the world.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.140
And from that, got to
be on the cover of Vogue

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.040
and go to the metal I mean, it's wild.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.780
- And I love that I found
out you were the first

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.600
little person ever got to
go to the Met Gala, right?

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:52.433
- Yeah.

00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:53.266
- That ever attended
and going to the carpet.

00:02:53.266 --> 00:02:54.220
How was that like?

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.220
I've never been.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.658
- An absolutely terrifying.

00:02:56.658 --> 00:02:57.600
(everyone laughing)

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.160
- They're all yelling at you,
like on the car?(laughing)

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.680
- It's so nerve wracking.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.750
But it was this extraordinary
thing where nobody

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.750
had ever been there, who
looked like me before.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.640
And if you can't see it, you can't be it.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.640
And for me, you woke up 23 steps,

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:11.950
it was talking about Anna Wintour,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.800
who's the editor and
chief of American Vogue,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.840
talking to her about
accessibility at the Met Galla.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.880
What does it look like
when we take an institution

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.920
like the Met, and make
it open to everyone?

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.160
So the Met Gala is an event
that happens once a year.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.200
It's a fundraiser for the
fashion department in the museum.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.210
And it is anybody who we
think we should be listening

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:32.130
to in the world, in many
ways are probably invited.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.330
And the tickets are ludicrously expensive.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:35.940
I went with a brand like Gucci

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.160
and Anna Wintour ticks off
everybody who gets to go.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.930
And to be there, I felt
a sense of pressure that,

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.040
whether I liked it or not,

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.120
despite only being able
to speak for myself,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.690
I was representing not just
people who look like me,

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:49.570
but people with disabilities in general.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.300
Because so often they're just
not part of our conversation.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.810
Even when we talk about
inclusion and diversity,

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.750
disability is not even
something or word that we use.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.600
- Because if people
aren't directly impacted

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.130
by it on an everyday basis,

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.180
it's not even the forefront of their mind.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.890
- It's not something we
think of and that ignorance

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.660
is often not malicious,
but that's not an excuse

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.380
for us not to be facilitating
the conversation.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.190
So where you go to
something like the Met Gala,

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:14.010
and you get to wear this magnificent dress

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.050
that's now on display in the
Gucci Garden and Florence,

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:21.540
what does the person who
sees that, sitting at home,

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.270
scrolling through Twitter and Instagram,

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.310
what do they now think
is possible for them?

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.960
And how long does it take
before the next little person

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.160
gets to go to the Met Galla?

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.030
I hope it's this year.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.220
- So how have you carried
this message though,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.230
to so many powerful people?

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.690
Did you just walk up?

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.150
I go to events, I've been in this industry

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.210
for nearly 20 years and I
still don't talk to people.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.010
I'm like, I ain't gonna
talk unless spoken to.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.701
Unless that freak Meryl
Streep at once, but--

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:43.940
(audience laughing)
Hey yeah.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.710
but I'm just saying that's
bold, just to walk up to people.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.030
- I live in a body where people are unsure

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.280
so often how to interact with me.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.020
And that discomfort can
sometimes bring about an apathy

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.660
and a conversation doesn't occur.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.660
So since I've been very, very young,

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.910
I've had to step into it and be proud

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.080
of the body that I live in.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.097
And yeah, I've walked up to Anna Wintour

00:05:04.097 --> 00:05:05.487
and the people I admire most, I'm like,

00:05:05.487 --> 00:05:07.987
"Hi, I'm Sinead, I think
your work is incredible.

00:05:07.987 --> 00:05:09.837
"I think we should have a conversation,

00:05:09.837 --> 00:05:12.000
"and I think we should talk
about design more broadly."

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.030
And in many ways, that's
probably what allows me

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.160
to do something like be a podcast host.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.350
I have a show called As Me with Sinead,

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.000
and I asked four questions
to every single guest,

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.920
including Jamie Lee Curtis,
Victoria Beckham, Dan Levy

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:26.910
and one of the questions I ask is,

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.330
what's it like to live in your body?

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.680
Because my experience is so explicit?

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.080
Well, most of the time, I
forget that I'm a little person

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.400
until I can't reach
something or I'm reminded,

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.860
and I think that's all of us.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.040
And what's the potential when we realize

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.980
that so many of our challenges
are not in isolation?

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.280
Like we all struggle.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.780
We all feel alone, we all
feel like we can't do things

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.660
or not represented and what happens

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.420
when we realize that,
so it is Kelly Clarkson,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.180
so it is Paul Wesley,
so it is Lilly Singh,

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.880
you're like, "oh, if they
can do it, why not me?"

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.703
- I mean--
(audience applauding)

00:06:03.186 --> 00:06:04.019
I love you.

00:06:04.962 --> 00:06:06.161
I just...

00:06:06.161 --> 00:06:06.994
I love when...

00:06:06.994 --> 00:06:09.520
I think what I love most is,
I mean I grew up in church

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.270
I just love hearing people preach and talk

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.840
and live something that
they're very passionate about

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:16.990
and they're very into and they're

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:19.469
so eloquently spoken at it as well,

00:06:19.469 --> 00:06:20.960
- it's so cool,
- Thank you.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.450
it's just inspiring and As
Me with Sinead is available

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.336
to download wherever
you can get your podcast

00:06:26.336 --> 00:06:29.990
and I am so into it, like
I'm in love with you.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.910
I think you're just such a great person,

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.410
you're speaking to like,
not only little people,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.470
you're speaking to the world
in a sense of breaking down

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.720
those barriers and I love
that, I think it's really...

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.110
we need to stop living in
fear and I agree with you.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:42.810
so thank ypu for that message, but

